<h3>Setting Up a Basic Pipeline</h3>

<ol>
  <li>
    <strong>Install dependencies</strong>
    <p>There is no Ollagraph SDK to install; the API is plain HTTPS with a Bearer key. The schema below uses Pydantic for validation.</p>
    <pre><code>pip install requests pydantic
export OLLAGRAPH_API_KEY="osk_..."</code></pre>
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Define the product schema</strong>
  </li>
</ol>

<pre><code>from enum import Enum
from typing import Optional

from pydantic import BaseModel, Field

class Availability(str, Enum):
    IN_STOCK = "in_stock"
    OUT_OF_STOCK = "out_of_stock"
    PRE_ORDER = "pre_order"
    BACKORDERED = "backordered"
    DISCONTINUED = "discontinued"

class Variant(BaseModel):
    sku: str = Field(pattern=r"^[A-Z0-9\-]&#123;4,20&#125;$")
    price: float = Field(gt=0, le=100000)
    currency: str = Field(pattern=r"^[A-Z]&#123;3&#125;$")
    compare_at_price: Optional[float] = None
    availability: Availability
    options: dict = &#123;&#125;

class Product(BaseModel):
    product_name: str
    brand: Optional[str] = None
    description: Optional[str] = None
    image_urls: list[str] = []
    variants: list[Variant] = Field(min_length=1)</code></pre>

<h3>Step 3: Fetch the page's structured data</h3>

<p>Ollagraph fetches the page and returns the structured data it embeds. Your code does the mapping.</p>

<pre><code>import os

import requests

API = "https://api.ollagraph.com"
HEADERS = &#123;"Authorization": f"Bearer &#123;os.environ['OLLAGRAPH_API_KEY']&#125;"&#125;

def fetch_structured(url):
    r = requests.post(f"&#123;API&#125;/v1/extract/structured", json=&#123;"url": url&#125;,
                      headers=HEADERS, timeout=60)
    r.raise_for_status()
    return r.json()  # json_ld, microdata, open_graph, meta, products</code></pre>

<h3>Step 4: Map JSON-LD to the schema and validate</h3>

<pre><code>AVAIL = &#123;"InStock": "in_stock", "OutOfStock": "out_of_stock", "PreOrder": "pre_order",
         "BackOrder": "backordered", "Discontinued": "discontinued"&#125;

def find_products(blocks):
    for block in blocks:
        for item in block.get("@graph", [block]):
            if item.get("@type") in ("Product", "ProductGroup"):
                yield item

def to_product(item):
    # A ProductGroup lists variants under hasVariant; a plain Product often
    # lists one offer per variant.
    variants = []
    for v in item.get("hasVariant") or [item]:
        offers = v.get("offers") or &#123;&#125;
        for o in offers if isinstance(offers, list) else [offers]:
            status = str(o.get("availability", "")).rsplit("/", 1)[-1]
            variants.append(&#123;
                "sku": o.get("sku") or v.get("sku"),
                "price": o.get("price"),
                "currency": o.get("priceCurrency"),
                "availability": AVAIL.get(status),  # unknown status fails validation
            &#125;)
    brand = item.get("brand")
    return Product(
        product_name=item.get("name"),
        brand=brand.get("name") if isinstance(brand, dict) else brand,
        variants=variants,
    )  # raises pydantic.ValidationError instead of storing bad data

data = fetch_structured("https://example.com/products/running-shoe")
for item in find_products(data["json_ld"]):
    product = to_product(item)
    print(f"Product: &#123;product.product_name&#125;")
    for v in product.variants:
        print(f"  &#123;v.sku&#125;: $&#123;v.price&#125; (&#123;v.availability.value&#125;)")</code></pre>

